### Linked List Basics: `Node` and `insert_at_head`

```c
typedef struct Node {
    int data;
    struct Node *next;
} Node;

Node *insert_at_head(Node *head, int value)
{
    Node *new_node = (Node *) malloc(sizeof(Node));
    if (new_node == NULL)
    {
        printf("Memory allocation failed for new node!\n");
        exit(1);
    }
    new_node->data = value;
    new_node->next = head;
    return new_node;
}

// ... main function calls ...
head = insert_at_head(head, 30);
head = insert_at_head(head, 20);
head = insert_at_head(head, 10);
printf("List after head insertions: ");
print_list(head); // Expected: 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> NULL
```

**Concept:** A `Node` holds data and a pointer to the next `Node`. `insert_at_head` adds a node to the front, updating the `head` pointer.

### Implementing `insert_at_end`: Step 1 - Create New Node

```c
Node *insert_at_end(Node *head, int value)
{
    Node *new_node = (Node *) malloc(sizeof(Node));
    if (new_node == NULL)
    {
        printf("Memory allocation failed for new node!\n");
        exit(1);
    }
    new_node->data = value;
    new_node->next = NULL; // This node will be the last

    // ... rest of the function ...
}
```

**Concept:** A new node is dynamically created. Its `data` is set, and its `next` pointer is initialized to `NULL` because it's intended to be the list's new tail.

### Implementing `insert_at_end`: Step 2 - Handle Empty List

```c
Node *insert_at_end(Node *head, int value)
{
    Node *new_node = (Node *) malloc(sizeof(Node));
    // ... (memory allocation and data/next setup) ...

    // If the list is empty, the new node becomes the head
    if (head == NULL)
    {
        return new_node;
    }

    // ... (traverse and link for non-empty list) ...
}
```

**Concept:** If the list is empty (`head == NULL`), the new node becomes the sole node and thus the new `head`.

### Implementing `insert_at_end`: Step 3 - Traverse to Last Node

```c
Node *insert_at_end(Node *head, int value)
{
    Node *new_node = (Node *) malloc(sizeof(Node));
    // ... (memory allocation, data/next setup, empty list check) ...

    // Traverse the list to find the last node
    Node *current = head;
    while (current->next != NULL)
    {
        current = current->next;
    }

    // ... (link new node) ...
}
```

**Concept:** A `current` pointer iterates through the list until `current->next` is `NULL`, indicating `current` is the last node.

### Implementing `insert_at_end`: Step 4 - Link and Return

```c
Node *insert_at_end(Node *head, int value)
{
    Node *new_node = (Node *) malloc(sizeof(Node));
    // ... (memory allocation, data/next setup, empty list check) ...

    // Traverse the list to find the last node
    Node *current = head;
    while (current->next != NULL)
    {
        current = current->next;
    }

    // Link the last node to the new node
    current->next = new_node;

    return head; // Return the original head (it hasn't changed for non-empty list)
}
```

**Concept:** The `next` pointer of the previously last node is updated to point to the new node, effectively appending it. The original `head` is returned.

### Testing `insert_at_end` in `main`

```c
int main(void)
{
    Node *head = NULL;

    // Insert some elements at the head
    head = insert_at_head(head, 30);
    head = insert_at_head(head, 20);
    head = insert_at_head(head, 10);
    printf("List after head insertions: ");
    print_list(head); // Expected: 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> NULL

    // Insert some elements at the end
    head = insert_at_end(head, 40);
    head = insert_at_end(head, 50);
    printf("List after end insertions: ");
    print_list(head); // Expected: 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> 40 -> 50 -> NULL

    // Test with an initially empty list for insert_at_end
    Node *empty_list_head = NULL;
    empty_list_head = insert_at_end(empty_list_head, 100);
    printf("Empty list after end insertion: ");
    print_list(empty_list_head); // Expected: 100 -> NULL
    free_list(empty_list_head);

    // Don't forget to free the main list!
    free_list(head);

    return 0;
}
```

**Concept:** Test cases cover both appending to an existing list and handling an initially empty list. Proper memory deallocation is crucial.

